 ],
 "literal": "The pure gold lotus worn by the bard, / along with the garland of the well-jeweled songstress, shining, / unharnessing the swift horses of the tall chariot and resting, / like people of a town, you stayed in the middle of the desert path. / 'Who are you?' asking unceasingly, / O suppliant with severe hunger and kinfolk like a dark cloud! / Before seeing the lord of the victorious spear, / we were indeed poorer than you; but now, / we have indeed become prosperous like this forever. / Even though knowing that it will neither wear nor wrap, / our king who gave a fine shawl to the shivering peacock, / Pegan of rutting elephants and spirited horses, / thinking 'giving is good, whatever the amount may be', / does not look to the next life indeed! / His generosity of hand looks only to the poverty of others.",
 "translation": "O suppliant, who travels with a large family like a dark cloud, suffering from severe hunger! You stopped in the middle of the desert path, unharnessed the swift horses of your tall chariot to rest, and stayed there like people settled in a town. When you unceasingly asked us, 'Who are you?', hear this: Before we saw our lord of the victorious spear, we were indeed poorer than you. But now, we have become prosperous like this forever. Our king, Pegan, who rules over rutting elephants and spirited horses, once gave his fine shawl to a shivering peacock, even though he knew well that a peacock can neither wear nor wrap itself in a garment. He does not give in order to gain rewards in the next life; his generous hand looks only to relieve the poverty of others.",
 "modern": "மேகக் கூட்டம் போன்ற சுற்றத்தாருடன் பசியால் வாடும் இரவலனே! நீ உனது நெடிய தேரின் குதிரைகளை அவிழ்த்துவிட்டு, பாலைவன வழியில் ஊர் மக்கள் போலத் தங்கியிருந்தாய். 'நீங்கள் யார்?' என்று எங்களை ஓயாமல் கேட்டாய். வெற்றியைத் தரும் வேலை ஏந்திய தலைவனைக் காண்பதற்கு முன்பு, நாங்கள் உன்னை விடவும் ஏழையாக இருந்தோம். ஆனால் இப்போது, எப்போதும் செல்வச் செழிப்புடன் விளங்குகிறோம். மயில் ஆடைகளை உடுத்தாது, போர்த்திக் கொள்ளாது என்று தெரிந்திருந்தும், குளிரால் நடுங்கிய மயிலுக்குத் தன் போர்வையைக் கொடுத்தவன் எங்கள் அரசன் பேகன். அவன் மதயானைகளையும் வேகமான குதிரைகளையும் உடையவன். 'எவ்வளவு கொடுத்தாலும் ஈகை செய்வதே நல்லது' என்று மறுமைப் பயனை எதிர்பார்த்து அவன் கொடுப்பதில்லை. அவனது கொடைத்தன்மை பிறருடைய வறுமையைப் போக்குவதை மட்டுமே நோக்கமாகக் கொண்டது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is a classic example of the Pāṇāṟṟuppaṭai genre, where a bard who has received wealth from a patron guides another poor bard to the same patron. The speaker meets a starving suppliant on a desert path who is resting with his family and chariot. The speaker explains that before meeting King Pegan, he was even poorer than the suppliant, but now he is highly prosperous. He praises Pegan's legendary act of giving his shawl to a shivering peacock, even though a peacock cannot wear clothes. The poet emphasizes that Pegan does not practice charity to earn spiritual merit for the next life, but solely out of pure compassion to relieve the poverty of others. Key phrases include 'மஞ்ஞைக்கு ஈத்த' (gave to the peacock) and 'மறுமை நோக்கின்றோ அன்றே' (does not look to the next life).",
